00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
when most people try and get a new job  they search for a listing  then they apply and then they wait  search apply  wait over and over again this is  all wrong i've hired over a hundred  people in my career  and today i'm going to explain exactly  how to get a new job  in just three weeks hunting for a new  job can feel daunting and believe me  i've been there but i promise you that  there is a right way  and a wrong way to go about finding a  new job the method i'm going to outline  doesn't just apply to finding a job it  works in sales  operations and even fundraising i've  actually used this exact technique  to raise over 100 million dollars for my  businesses if you're new here i'm john  i live in los angeles california and  have been an entrepreneur for the past  decade  every week i put out new videos about  technology startups so please hit that  subscribe button and ring the bell so  you don't miss any let me walk you  through how most people apply for jobs  and then explain why it takes forever  and rarely gets results  first you start searching around for job  postings casually bouncing between  websites until you find something that  looks interesting then you read the post  and see if you could qualify  usually the job description is extremely  long and vague with dozens of  qualifications that sometimes contradict  one another then after you finally find  something that looks good  you fill out an application and start to  wait if you don't hear back  you start to look for another job  listing but this is extremely slow  then even after you've applied you have  to wade through several rounds of phone  screens and interviews  to find out if you even got the job i  see people doing this all the time  and it always takes them forever to land  a new job this can be really dangerous  because  for better or worse lots of human  resource departments are trained to  filter out candidates with resume gaps  meaning that more than a few months have  passed between jobs  following this process of search apply  interview is terrible  and can lead to a downward spiral where  it actually gets harder  the longer you work at it but there's a  much better way essentially  instead of doing one step after the  other you want to do  everything in parallel that means  instead of just searching for a single  job posting  you need to collect hundreds of postings  before you fill out a single application  getting a job is just like selling a  product it's impossible to have a 100  conversion rate so you need to  acknowledge that up front  apple has one of the best brands in the  world and some really great marketing to  push their products  but not every apple advertisement is  going to lead to a customer purchase  so they spread their bets out and market  their products broadly  let's break the entire job hunting  process down into three key steps  which could be done one week at a time  first up is what i call research and  development and this is what happens  before you apply to a single job posting  this whole process is about being as  efficient as possible  so it's important to do all of your  research in parallel  your goal here is to build a massive  list of prospects  that you can reach out to in step two so  organization is key  instead of searching for job postings in  this stage you need to search for  companies and more specifically  individual people who have the authority  to hire you  start by listing out every company that  operates in whatever industry you were  just working in  then expand out based on your specific  skill set so if you've been working in  marketing at an electronics company  start by finding as many electronics  companies as possible  then broadening out to any company with  a growing marketing department  don't try and filter too much at this  stage instead just focus on gathering as  much information as possible  and compiling everything in a  spreadsheet if you're used to using a  tool like notion or airtable  you can definitely use those but google  sheets is fine too  just make sure to note the name of the  company and how big they are  working for a small company can be  dramatically different than working for  a large corporation  so it's worthwhile to keep size in mind  you can usually find out how many  employees a particular company has by  searching linkedin  or just google applying for jobs is  really a sales process  and not every lead is going to convert  into a job offer so you want to maximize  your top of funnel  leads by collecting as many potential  opportunities as possible  it's not crazy to compile a list of over  a hundred potential companies before you  start applying  if you spend five minutes researching  each company you should be able to  compile a list of a hundred companies in  just one day once you have all these  companies in your spreadsheet  start developing your leads this is  where you start to find job listings and  contact  information for managers who hire people  with your skills  you should be adding information to each  row in your spreadsheet  so you wind up with names and contact  information for every target company  this is also a good time to start  developing your personal elevator pitch  as you research these companies and  start seeing patterns in what they're  hiring for  try to imagine how you would fit into  the organization there's often a big  mismatch between the title of the job  and the description below  at google a software engineer might be  expected to have a computer science  degree  and really understand algorithms whereas  a small company  outside the tech industry might still be  hiring a software engineer but  really just needs someone who can build  a simple website you want to figure out  what skills you have and what skills are  immediately adjacent to your experience  if you feel like you're lacking a  specific skill now is a great time to go  and learn more  if you've been programming for a few  years but don't really have any  experience with design  take time before your job search fully  gets going to try and get up to speed  with the basics of that discipline  you can't become an expert in just one  week but it never hurts to be more  well-rounded then comes step  two parallel outreach this is really the  heart of this entire process  hr departments are notoriously slow to  process job applications  and you never want to be in a position  where you're just waiting to hear back  about a job  and have nothing to do in a great  scenario maybe 10  of job applications are going to turn  into interviews so you really need to  fill up your lead spreadsheet before you  start reaching out  the goal is to only go through this  process once and then just decide  between multiple job offers at the end  but you can obviously repeat the cycle  if need be the key is to keep the top  of the funnel as big as possible so  every stage has the maximum chance of  converting  applying to multiple jobs can be really  daunting and it's not uncommon to think  that you should just focus all of your  energy on a single posting  and spend an entire week preparing for  that but that's a huge mistake  the first stage of any hiring process is  basically just a filter  you might be the perfect candidate for a  position and spend  weeks preparing an amazing application  only to find out that they just hired  someone a week ago  and forgot to close out the job listing  so you need to standardize your process  for applying  you don't want to spam people but a  little bit of process optimization can  go a long way here  after all if you're serious about  applying to a hundred jobs in one go  you can't spend much time on each one so  you need to create a workflow for  efficiency  you can use a tool like mail merge to  automatically fill out personalized  emails  but sometimes this can backfire even if  you add personal details like the name  of the person you're emailing  it can still feel like a marketing email  where there were clearly just a few  holes that got filled in automatically  instead you should have a few lines of  boilerplate about yourself  and then customize the rest in your  spreadsheet you should have  the name of the company and the name of  the person you're reaching out to which  is a good starting point  if you have time be sure to add in a few  details about the position and why you  want to work for the company  this is a great place to show that you  did some research and mention a recent  announcement  by the company that you couldn't have  possibly just autofilled  the key here is to stick to a schedule  if your target is to apply to a hundred  companies in a single week you shouldn't  spend more than 25 minutes per company  that should be plenty of time if you're  well organized and have all of your  supporting materials  like a resume and links to relevant work  experience all together already in one  place  as you're applying really try and figure  out what steps you have to repeat over  and over again so you can automate them  tools like auto hotkey and textexpander  can create macros to keep you from  typing the same sentence  over and over again regardless of  whether you use mac  or pc there are great options to speed  up your process  and i'll leave some links to helpful  resources right below the like button  step 3 is what i call targeted  interviewing it's easy to treat every  interview the same way  and put your maximum effort into each  one but that's actually a common error  interviewing for a new job should be as  much about you deciding whether or not  you want the job  as the company deciding whether or not  they want to hire you  job postings can be very misleading and  it's not always the company's fault the  hiring manager could be using an old  template or the company's goals could  have shifted  since they put up the job rack either  way you can't fall into the trap of  thinking that you have to see  every interview process to the end far  too many managers would love to hire  someone who's a bit overqualified  and you don't want to end up being that  someone it's important to validate early  on in the interview process  exactly what you'll be doing and assess  whether or not that's a fit for you  you might have done a particular job a  few years ago and hated it  so even though you're technically  qualified you need to filter out any  companies that want you to do that  terrible  job there are two added benefits to this  more aggressive approach  first taking an active interest in the  position and really digging in to see if  it's what you want to be doing for the  next few years  shows a lot of initiative and that looks  great in interviews  second declining to advance to the next  round after a phone screen reveals that  a job isn't quite right for you  builds your confidence if you followed  the first two steps properly  you should have plenty of interviews  going on simultaneously  and never feel like you have to take a  job because it's the only opportunity  available  this strategy is all about parallelism  and if you do it right  you'll wind up in a great situation  where you can actually decide between  multiple job offers and weigh the pros  and cons of each one  you might even be able to negotiate for  a higher salary at the most interesting  company  because you have some real leverage even  though companies are legally limited in  what they can ask you about your job  search  most managers can actually sense when a  candidate that they really want to hire  has multiple offers even if you don't  say it explicitly  so use this to your advantage if you'd  like to go a bit deeper  just drop your email at johncoogan.com  and please check out this recommended  video  the youtube algorithm thinks that you'll  really like it

